le. But the action \"Chi\" is only allowed if it completes a Chow, not necessarily a winning hand. So I can do it. After Chi, I would have to discard one tile. So I would have to choose which tile to discard from my remaining hand: either W4 or T2. Then I would be in tenpai (ready) waiting for a tile to complete the pair. Which tile would I need? After Chi, my hand tiles become: from original hand [W4, T2, T4, T5], I use T4 and T5 to form Chow with discarded T6, so I remove T4 and T5, add the Chow to melds. Then I have W4 and T2 left. I must discard one. If I discard W4, I keep T2, then I need another T2 to make a pair. If I discard T2, I keep W4, then I need another W4 to make a pair. So I would be waiting for either T2 or W4. Let's check the unshown tiles count: W4: 4 left, T2: 3 left. So both are available. But which is better? Considering scoring patterns: I already have melds: Chow of W1-2-3, Chow of W7-8-9, Pung of J2. If I add Chow of T4-5-6, then my hand consists of: three chows and one pung. The pair will be either T2 or W4. Possible patterns: All Chows (if pair is suited) \u2013 but I have a pung, so not All Chows. Maybe Half Flush? I have honor tiles (J2 pung) and suited tiles of two suits: W and T. Half Flush requires only one suit and honors. I have two suits, so not Half Flush. All Types? I have W, T, and honors (J2), but no B. So not All Types. All Simples? My tiles: W1,2,3,7,8,9 \u2013 includes terminals (1,9), so not All Simples. Outside Hand? Requires terminals/honors in every meld and pair. My melds: W1-2-3 (has terminal W1), W7-8-9 (has terminal W9), J2 pung (honor), T4-5-6 (no terminal/honor). So not Outside Hand. Mixed Straight? I have chows in different suits? W1-2-3 and W7-8-9 are same suit, T4-5-6 is different. Mixed Straight requires three chows of 123, 456, 789 in different suits. I have 123 in W, 456? T4-5-6 is 456 in T, and 789 in W. So two chows in W, one in T. Not three different suits. So Mixed Straight not satisfied. Pure Straight? R