 discarding B1, you have 4 tiles: W9, T2, T4, T5. To win, you draw a tile that either (a) completes a Chow and pairs one of the leftovers, or (b) pairs one tile and completes a Chow with the others. Let's list:  \n  - Draw T3: then you have Chow T2-T3-T4, leftover W9 and T5. Not a pair. So not winning.  \n  - Draw T6: Chow T4-T5-T6, leftover W9 and T2. Not a pair.  \n  - Draw W9: pair W9, leftover T2, T4, T5. Need Chow: T2-T3-T4 (needs T3) or T3-T4-T5 (needs T3) or T4-T5-T6 (needs T6). Not winning immediately.  \n  - Draw T2: pair T2, leftover W9, T4, T5. Need Chow: only possible is T4-T5-T6 (needs T6) or W9 with something? No.  \n  - Draw T4: pair T4, leftover W9, T2, T5. No Chow possible.  \n  - Draw T5: pair T5, leftover W9, T2, T4. No Chow possible.  \nSo discarding B1 does not put you in tenpai.  \n\n- Discard T5: hand becomes W9, T2, T4, B1. Possible melds: Chow T2-T3-T4 (needs T3). Pair could be W9 or B1 or T2 or T4. Waiting tiles:  \n  - Draw T3: Chow T2-T3-T4, leftover W9 and B1. Not a pair.  \n  - Draw W9: pair W9, leftover T2, T4, B1. Need Chow: only T2-T3-T4 (needs T3).  \n  - Draw B1: pair B1, leftover W9, T2, T4. No Chow.  \n  - Draw T2: pair T2, leftover W9, T4, B1. No Chow.  \n  - Draw T4: pair T4, leftover W9, T2, B1. No Chow.  \nNot tenpai.  \n\n- Discard T2: hand becomes W9, T4, T5, B1. Possible melds: Chow T4-T5-T6 (needs T6). Pair could be W9 or B1 or T4 or T5. Waiting tiles:  \n  - Draw T6: Chow T4-T5-T6, leftover W9 and B1. Not a pair.  \n  - Draw W9: pair W9, leftover T4, T5, B1. Need Chow: T4-T5-T6 (needs T6).  \n  - etc. Not tenpai.  \n\n- Discard W9: hand becomes T2, T4, T5, B1. Possible melds: Chow T2-T3-T4 (needs T3) or Chow T4-T5-T6 (needs T6). Pair could be B1 or T2 or T4 or T5. Waiting tiles:  \n  - Draw T3: Chow T2-T3-T4, leftover T5 and B1. Not a pair.  \n  - Draw T6: Chow T4-T5-T6, leftover T2 and B1. Not a pair.  \n  - Draw B1: pair B1, leftover T2, T4, T5. Need Chow: T2-T3-T4 (needs T3) or T4-T5-T6 (needs T6).  \n  - etc. Not tenpai.  \n\n- Discard T4: hand becomes W9, T2, T5, B1. Possible melds? No Chow possible because T2 and T5 are not consecutive. So only possibility is to have a pair and a pung? But you need a meld, which could be a pung if you draw two more of something, but that's not tenpai. So not tenpai.  \n\nNone of the discards put you in tenpai with a single waiting tile. That means you are not actually in tenpai; you have 14 tiles but need to discard one to have 13 tiles, and you are not ready yet. So you must improve your hand. Looking at the unseen tiles count: T3 has 4 left, T6 has 2 left. You have many T3 available. Perhaps you should keep T2, T4, T5 to try to complete a Chow. Discarding B1 or W9 might be best to keep flexibility. But also consider scoring: you already have a Pung of J2 (Dragon Pung worth 2 points) and possibly other patterns. Your melds include Chow W1-W2-W3 and W7-W8-W9, which are terminal chows? W1-W2-W3 is terminal chow? W1 is terminal, but W3 is not terminal? Terminal chows are 1-2-3 and 7-8-9. So you have both terminal chows in W suit. That could be \"Two Terminal Chows\" worth 1 point. Also, you have no honors except J2, so \"No Honors\" might apply? But J2 is an honor, so no. \"All Simples\"? No, because you have W1, W9, J2. So not.  \n\nGiven the unseen tiles, T3 is abundant (4 left), so keeping T2 and T4 is good to wait for T3. But you also have T5, which could combine with T4 for T6. So maybe keep T2, T4, T5 and discard B1 or W9. Discarding B1 seems safe because B1 is a terminal, and there are 2 left unseen, but it's not a great tile to keep. Discarding W9: you already have a W9 in your meld (Chow W7-W8-W9), so keeping a single W9 might be useful for a pair, but you have no other W9. Unseen W9: 1 left. So low chance.  \n\nI think the best discard is B1 because it's a terminal and not connected to anything. But let's check opponents' melds: next player has T1 pung, across has W4 pung, previous has B5 pung. Many terminals and honors are already used. B1 might be relatively safe. Also, from history, B1 has been played twice (by across and previous), so maybe safer.  \n\nReason: After analyzing, I am not in tenpai. I need to discard a tile to improve hand toward tenpai. Discarding B1 keeps the T2, T4, T5 sequence which has good potential to form a Chow with T3 or T6, and keeps W9 as a possible pair. T3 has 4 unseen, good probability. So discard B1.  \n\nAnswer: Play B1"}
